export default {"id":"djing-level-1","number":1,"title":"The Modern DJ and Academy Stack","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"At working level, DJing is continuous control of three variables: 1. Selection — which recording plays next, and which recordings are excluded","modules":[{"id":"djing-m-1-1","code":"1","title":"The Modern DJ and Academy Stack","blurb":"At working level, DJing is continuous control of three variables: 1. Selection — which recording plays next, and which recordings are excluded","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"djing-l-o","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"overview","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module 1 — The Modern DJ and Academy Stack","level":1},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Objective:</strong> Define the professional task of DJing, fix the hardware and software stack, and state edition and sister-course boundaries.   <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate foundation — two full lessons.   <strong>Sources:</strong> <em>How-to-DJ Ableton Live 12 Standard Knowledgebase</em> §1–2; <em>Foundations of Digital DJing</em>; <em>DJing Architecture with Ableton Live 12</em>; Live 12 Standard Manual (Session View); Berklee Online public DJ skill sequence; academy edition lock (Standard + Operator + Max for Live · Launchkey MK4 61 · Banana-first cue path)."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"djing-l-1-1-1","title":"1.1.1 — Definition of DJing: Task, Modes, Core Skills","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Primary controls","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"At working level, DJing is continuous control of three variables:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Selection</strong> — which recording plays next, and which recordings are excluded  ","<strong>Timing</strong> — tempo alignment, phase (shared downbeats), and phrase alignment (section changes coincide)  ","<strong>Energy</strong> — intensity contour over time (not continuous maximum level)"],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Definition:</strong> DJing is the selection and temporal joining of recordings so that the resulting sequence is continuous rather than a series of isolated starts and stops."},{"type":"prose","html":"Subsequent modules apply this definition to Ableton Live 12 Standard, Launchkey MK4 61, Windows 11, and Voicemeeter Banana Main/Cue routing."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Performance modes","blocks":[{"type":"table","headers":["Mode","Primary activity"],"rows":[["<strong>DJ set</strong>","Selection and blending of pre-recorded tracks in real time"],["<strong>Live electronic set</strong>","Performance of original parts and clips as an instrument"],["<strong>Hybrid</strong>","Pre-recorded tracks combined with live layers (loops, one-shots, limited keyboard material)"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"This course prioritizes the DJ set. Hybrid performance is introduced only in Module 13, after two-deck technique is established. Session View is the performance surface. Arrangement View is used for recording sets and for editing, not as the default deck surface (Live Manual: Session View supports cases in which order and duration are not fixed in advance)."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Historical context","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Development sequence for context only: turntables and mixer → CDJs and media players → controller and dual-deck software → DAW performance (Ableton Session View). The path taught here is Session View with Launchkey. Mixxx is an optional end unit after the capstone, not a parallel core path."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Core skills","blocks":[{"type":"table","headers":["Skill","Definition","Initial practice stance"],"rows":[["<strong>Beatmatching</strong>","Shared tempo and phase so percussion, especially kicks, forms one pulse","Develop auditory lock; tempo sync is introduced later and does not replace detection of incorrect warps (<em>How-to-DJ</em> §2.1)"],["<strong>Phrasing</strong>","Incoming section changes coincide with outgoing section changes","In much 4/4 dance music a common phrase unit is 8 bars (32 beats); hip-hop, pop, and radio edits often use shorter structures — structure is confirmed by listening, not by formula alone"],["<strong>Cueing</strong>","Store a start point; preview on the cue path; match; wait for phrase boundary; enter the mix","Sequence: set cue → cue bus → match → phrase boundary → open into the mix"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"Public Berklee Online DJ syllabus materials order fundamentals similarly: drop on beat 1 → beatmatching → headphone monitoring → phrase mixing → EQ and filter → then grids, sync, and effects. Host software changes; the skill order remains applicable."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Course end state","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"On completion of the capstone the student can: launch multiple warped full tracks in Session View under control; cue the next track while the room path carries only the main mix; enter on phrase with aligned kicks; manage low-frequency content so concurrent full bass lines do not produce mud; keep main output headroom appropriate at the physical output; deliver planned short sets and a multi-genre capstone set. Suite edition features are not required."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Common errors","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Treating DJing as playlist curation with occasional fader movement  ","Using tempo sync to conceal unwarped or incorrectly warped material  ","Applying fixed phrase formulas while ignoring genre structure  ","Introducing hybrid layers or effects before clean two-deck technique is established  "],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Laboratory 1.1.1","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Write a half-page brief defining the three primary controls; identify the weakest of the three core skills; state one weekly drill for that skill.  ","Select one recorded set used as a reference. Map it approximately as open → build → peak → cool using energy language rather than tempo alone.  ","Name two topics this course does not cover (sister courses or Suite-only features)."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Pass criteria:</strong> Primary controls, core skills, and hybrid definition stated accurately in under 90 seconds without notes. The written brief is specific."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"djing-l-1-2-1","title":"1.2.1 — Locked Stack, Sister Courses, Scope Rules","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1.2.1 — Locked Stack, Sister Courses, Scope Rules","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Stack roles","level":3},{"type":"table","headers":["Component","Role on this path"],"rows":[["<strong>Live 12 Standard</strong>","Decks, mixer, and effects; primary live work in Session View"],["<strong>Warp (Complex / Complex Pro)</strong>","Full songs follow Set tempo (Manual: Complex/Pro for polyphonic or full-song material; higher CPU cost)"],["<strong>Crossfader and channel faders</strong>","Blend control (Manual: any number of tracks assignable A/B; seven curves)"],["<strong>EQ Three / EQ Eight / Utility</strong>","Low-frequency management, tone, and trim"],["<strong>Main Out and Cue Out</strong>","Room path and headphone path (Manual: distinct outputs required; Solo/Cue mode set to Cue)"],["<strong>Launchkey MK4 61</strong>","Clip and scene launch, levels, limited EQ/filter mapping; MPE input off for multi-channel DJ routing"],["<strong>Windows 11 + ASIO</strong>","Real-time host path into Live"],["<strong>Voicemeeter Banana</strong>","Primary multi-output so Live can present Main and Cue as separate pairs"]],"caption":null},{"type":"prose","html":"Operator and Max for Live are present on the academy stack and appear only in limited hybrid work later. Deep synthesis and MIDI production belong to sister courses."},{"type":"heading","text":"Cue path policy","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Primary:</strong> Banana multi-out into Live ASIO.   <strong>Alternate:</strong> multi-out hardware interface (for example Main 1/2, Cue 3/4).   <strong>Scope note:</strong> No single official Ableton documentation entry specifies an authoritative Live 12 and Banana channel map. Channel indices used in practice must be verified by listening (<em>How-to-DJ</em> known uncertainties)."},{"type":"heading","text":"Features not taught as owned","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Stem Separation, Sampler, Wavetable, Meld, Roar, Echo, Hybrid Reverb, Amp/Cabinet, and other Suite-only workflows. Training PDFs may list Suite devices alongside Standard material; listing is not ownership."},{"type":"heading","text":"Sister course boundaries","level":3},{"type":"table","headers":["Sister course","Scope owned there"],"rows":[["Mastering Ableton Live 12","Full device reference, Arrangement production, Operator depth, Max for Live depth"],["Learning the Launchkey MK4 61","Hardware depth, Components, Windows MIDI, mode reference"],["Foundations of Music Production","Session and Arrangement songwriting; remix as composition"],["Drums and Rhythm Programming","Programming grooves; this course only launches loops as layers"],["Sound Design Foundations","Synthesis literacy; hybrid keyboard use here remains limited"]],"caption":null},{"type":"heading","text":"Library ethics","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"Crates are built from material the student has the right to use. Piracy and unauthorized distribution paths are not taught."},{"type":"heading","text":"Capstone preview","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"A 30–45 minute multi-genre recorded set, written set map, and self-critique against gates A–I."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Pedagogical sequence (do not invert):</strong> auditory skills before tempo sync; clean transitions before effects; energy architecture before key-based devices; pure two-deck craft before hybrid layers."},{"type":"heading","text":"Laboratory 1.2.1","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Confirm Live edition is Standard (or higher while still teaching the Standard-only path).  ","Confirm Launchkey is recognized by Live as a control surface (detailed mapping in Module 8).  ","Write a one-page stack card: Main audio path, Cue audio path, MIDI path, sister-course exclusion list, three Suite features excluded from core.  ","Open Live and Session View; state each locked stack component."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Pass criteria:</strong> Stack card is accurate for the machine in use. Banana-first cue policy and two sister-course boundaries stated without reference notes."}]},{"type":"step","title":"Module 1 checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Three primary controls and three core skills explained as a system  ","[ ] Locked stack and Banana-first cue path stated correctly  ","[ ] Sister courses and Suite exclusions named  ","[ ] Capstone end state and pedagogical sequence stated  "],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
